{-
   Runs of slots - Implementation-Containers.md §2.
-}

{-
   How a stored count is represented, and why it is neither `Int` nor a whole word.

   **Unsigned**, because a count has no negative values and the sign bit is the only thing signedness
   buys it. What that is worth is not the extra bit: it is that `index >= length` on unsigned
   operands is the *whole* bounds test, since a negative index converted to this type is a very large
   one and fails the same comparison. Two tests become one everywhere a container checks an index,
   which is the same trick every bounds check in every systems language uses and the reason JS
   specifies `.length` as a `uint32` rather than as a number that happens not to go below zero.

   **Thirty bits and not thirty-two**, because a capacity shares its word with the two bits below
   and thirty is what is left. Those bits are the whole of the difference between this cap and a
   `U32`'s: 1073741823 elements is one gibibyte of `[U8]` and eight of `[Int]`, since the bound is on
   the element count rather than on the byte size. A `resize` past it fails in the same way one the
   allocator refused does, and lifting it properly means a wider count, which is a Repr variant
   (Implementation-Containers.md §9) rather than a bit that can be found somewhere.

   A plain `alias` and not `alias qualified`: this is a width, not a new type, and every `Int` that
   reaches it should convert by writing `::` rather than by unwrapping a newtype.
-}
{-
   How wide a *borrowed* container's length is, and why it is not `Count`.

   `Count` below is what an owner *stores*, and §9's whole design is that an owner's representation
   varies - per implementation, per target, per root. A **borrow** is the opposite: a window is the
   single representation every `[T]` signature in every program shares, so whatever it can describe
   is the ceiling for every container that will ever be passed as `[T]`. A thirty-one-bit borrow
   would make the *universal* thing the limiting one, which inverts §1 - an owner could be as large
   as it liked and simply never be borrowable, which is what a `LargeArray` would have run into.

   So a slice's length is `Size`, Core's word-width index type - see the note where it is bound. The
   cost natively is a sign-extension where an `Int` index meets a `Size` bound, and four bytes on a
   *stored* slice; both are named in Implementation-Containers.md §4.4.
-}

pub alias Count = @bits(30) U32

{-
   What releasing and growing this run mean, as the two questions that are actually asked.

   `InstAlloc::storageFlag` chooses between four storage classes and this records none of them:
   nothing ever asks *which* class - `releaseRun` asks "is this mine to hand back", and inline,
   frame and region storage answer no for three different reasons that are none of a run's business.

   What is asked, and what one bit could not say, is the *second* question. `resize` relocates by
   replacing `items`, and a run whose slots are its owner's own bytes has no `items` to replace -
   Implementation-Containers.md §7.1's `@inline(i) @capacity(i)` stores no pointer at all, so the
   descriptor a borrow of one materializes is a temporary and a write into it would be a write into
   nothing. `runFixed` is that fact, said once here rather than tested for at each of the three
   sites that could relocate: such a run refuses to grow, which is also §7.1's "`@capacity(n)` is
   enforced, not masked".

   So two bits and thirty of capacity. What is lost is a printed IR that named the storage class.
   The escape analysis still decides between four; it writes the answer to these questions rather
   than the decision itself.
-}
pub alias HeapFlag = @bits(2) U32

-- The largest count either field can hold, as the number a caller is checked against rather than as
-- a mask applied behind its back. `@bits` stores truncate silently, which is tolerable for an
-- integer and corrupts a container for a length - Implementation-Containers.md §7.1.
pub let maxCount = 1073741823 :: Size

{-
   How many bytes `count` elements occupy.

   Written as a pointer difference rather than with `sizeOf`, because what is wanted is the size of
   a *type* and pointer arithmetic already scales by exactly that - `from` is never read, only
   measured against.

   **`Size` on both sides**, and not `I64` on the result: a count and a byte quantity are the same
   kind of number here, because a byte count is bounded by the address space and so is the width of
   a pointer by definition rather than sixty-four bits by habit - Analysis-Modules.md §4.2.1's
   second row. Writing that into the type is what makes a thirty-two-bit target compute this in one
   register instead of two.
-}
pub fn byteSpan(from: %a, count: Size) -> Size =
    difference(bitcast(from) :: %U8, bitcast(from + count) :: %U8)

{-
   An owned, uninitialized run of slots, plus where it came from.

   Two words and no policy. It does not know which of its slots hold values, it cannot be pushed
   to, and it will not grow on its own - which is the whole point: occupancy is a private matter for
   whichever container is built on top of it, so a count, a bitmap, a sentinel and a free list are
   all equally expressible and none of them is in the primitive.

   `ownsHeap` is what the compiler decided about where these slots live, reduced to the one question
   anyone asks of it - see InstAlloc::storageFlag and `HeapFlag`. It is the only thing here the
   program did not write, and it exists so that `Reclaim` below is a test rather than a guess: the
   same two words describe a run inlined into its owner, one on the frame, one in a region and one
   from the allocator, and only the last of those is anyone's to hand back.

   Two words and not three, because the capacity and that bit share one, and the bit is deliberately
   at the *end* of the word rather than in the pointer. The low bits of `items` would have held it
   and would have bought a thirty-second bit of count, at the price of a `%a` that is not an address:
   `items + index` is the most frequent operation on this field, a low tag does not survive it at a
   stride of one, and every run would have to be over-aligned to make room. See
   Implementation-Containers.md §10.3.
-}
@builtin(run)
pub data Run(a) {items: %a, capacity: Count, ownsHeap: HeapFlag}

-- The three answers, for the reason `HeapFlag` gives. `runBorrowed` covers frame and region storage
-- together: each is handed back by something that is not this run - the frame returning, the region
-- closing - and telling those two apart would be a distinction nothing acts on. `runFixed` is the
-- one that is not about release at all: the slots are an owner's own bytes, so nothing hands them
-- back *and* nothing may move them.
pub let runBorrowed = 0 :: HeapFlag
pub let runFromHeap = 1 :: HeapFlag
pub let runFixed = 2 :: HeapFlag

-- A run with room for nothing, which allocates nothing. Every container's empty value starts here.
pub fn emptyRun() -> Run(a) = Run {items: null(), capacity: 0, ownsHeap: runBorrowed}

{-
   **`newRun` is declared per target, in `Run.native.yana` and `Run.js.yana`, and not here.**

   It claims one role in both, which is the arrangement `blockCopy`/`copyMemory` already uses: a role
   claimed by declarations on opposite sides of a platform filter is claimed exactly once in every
   build. What differs is that the native one also carries a *body*, and a body beside a role is not
   a contradiction - the hook is what a call site expands to and the body is what a call that cannot
   be expanded reaches.

   `compiler/resolve` expanded both, and the expansion is the one operation the language has no way
   to say about itself: an ordinary allocation with a count beside it (`InstAlloc::extent`), so
   where the slots lived was decided by the same escape analysis that placed every other allocation
   and the bit it wrote into `ownsHeap` was that decision reduced to what a run acts on. The
   resolver that remains has no placement analysis, so on native it calls the body, which allocates
   from the heap and says so in the tag; the body has to be native because a heap is what it asks
   for, which is why the declaration could not stay here.
-}

{-
   **There is no `capacity(self: Run(a))` any more**, and `self.capacity :: Size` at the handful of
   sites that asked is what took its place.

   `doc/spec/overloading.md` R1 keys an overload set by `(name, arity)` and admits at most one plain
   function in it; `Array(a)` has the other one, so a module importing both got "ambiguous name
   capacity" for any program that wrote the word. The array's is the one that stays, because it is
   the one a *program* calls - a run is reached only from this module and `Core/Array.native.yana`,
   and both have the field in hand.

   A class over the two was the first answer and does not work yet, which is worth writing down
   because it looks like the obvious one (it is `doc/progress/2026-09-06-inventory.md` D5). A class
   member's subject for a container is the container's **view** - a shared
   `[a]` in a binding position is two words - and a capacity is a fact about the *owner*, which the
   view does not carry: `instance Capacity(Array(a))` reports "a shared view of Array(a) has no field
   called run" there, whatever `self` is written as. What gets a member the owner is a `@builtin`
   role on the class, the way `Viewed` and `Contiguous` do, and that is compiler machinery rather
   than something a library declares. Worth revisiting when a class can ask for the owner.
-}

-- Where the slots start. A container indexes off this; nothing here says how many of them hold
-- anything, because a run does not know. The result is a pointer into the run's storage, which is
-- what `%source'a` says and what keeps the run alive for as long as the pointer is.
pub fn slots(self: source'Run(a)) -> %source'a = self.items

{-
   The representations - Implementation-Containers.md §3.

   A container author needs access to a run's contents, and a single raw pointer cannot serve: a
   narrow-element run has no element address, and a run whose count was packed into its owner has no
   self-contained descriptor to point at. So the shapes are named types, each with a coherent
   operation set, and an author dispatches on the representation rather than testing for it - a
   UTF-8 decoder takes a `[U8]` window, a bitset scan will take a `Bits(Bool)`.

   The window is also what a borrow of `[a]` *is* - `ArrayView(a)`. That is not two facts: §4's
   slice Repr and that record are the same shape named twice, which is why the borrow of a
   container never dispatches. `Bits(a)` is the narrow-element form and waits on §11's stride.

   It owns nothing. Every field is a copy of something whose lifetime belongs to whoever the slice
   was taken from, which is what makes it TrivialCopy and what the borrow at the point it was made
   is responsible for.
-}
{-
   Two shapes, and the third field is what a target with no addresses costs -
   Implementation-Containers.md §4.3 and §14.

   Natively a window into a run is a *shifted base*: `items + start` is an address like any other, so
   two words describe it. A host array cannot be shifted - `arr.slice(a, b)` copies and there is no
   `subarray` outside the typed families - so a window there is the array plus where in it the window
   begins, and §4.3's three-component slice is what that is. Nothing above the field list changes:
   both are the one representation every `[T]` signature shares on its own target, and both are
   TrivialCopy.

   `offset` is last rather than second: `items` and `length` keep one position on both targets.
   That was load-bearing while `compiler/resolve` read the fields by position; the compiler reads
   no field of either record now, so the order is kept rather than needed.

   The record is `ArrayView` in `Core/Array.native.yana` and `Core/Array.js.yana`, on the terms §2.5
   gives: two field lists, and `values` on the target that has an address to hand out.
-}

{-
   Subscripting a slice and a raw pointer - Core's `Index`, and where `xs[i]` on an array ends up.

   **Written in `Core` now; `compiler/resolve` generated them, and this is what it generated:**

       @platform(native) instance Index('[a], Size, a):
           fn get(self: '[a], index: Size) -> 'a = borrow(self.items + index)
           fn getMut(&self: '[a], index: Size) -> &a = borrowMut(self.items + index)

       @platform(js) instance Index('[a], Size, a):
           fn get(self: '[a], index: Size) -> 'a = hostAt(self.items, self.offset + index)
           fn getMut(&self: '[a], index: Size) -> &a = hostAtMut(self.items, self.offset + index)

       instance Index(%a, I64, a):
           fn get(self: %source'a, index: I64) -> source'a = borrow(self + index)
           fn getMut(self: %source'a, index: I64) -> &source'a = borrowMut(self + index)

   All six now say the same thing the same way, and the pointer pair is where that took work. A
   slice is a reference, so the tick on its type says which loan the result is in
   (Analysis-Borrows.md §4.5); a pointer is not, and the older spelling reached for the `return`
   marker because `'%a` reads as a reference *to* a pointer rather than a pointer to a reference.
   The tick is structural, so the answer was to write it where every other tick goes - on the
   constructor's argument. `%source'a` is a pointer to storage in `source`, `source'(%a)` is the
   pointer field one would have got by writing it outside, and the marker is gone.

   Each of those is one address computation with nothing behind it, and every one of them is
   expressible here - so leaving them in source was the default and the reason that stage did not
   was worth stating. `xs[i]` is the most common expression the language has, and it has to cost
   nothing *with no optimizer having run*: a source body would have to be specialized per element
   type and then spliced at every subscript in the program before it was free, so a `-no-opt` build
   would pay for a call at each one and the shape of the emitted code would depend on a pass rather
   than on what was written. Nothing above the optimizer is allowed to depend on the optimizer, and
   the language's most common operation is the last place to start. Its generated body
   (`defineNativeIndexInstances`) and the expansion a call site got were the same emitter.

   **The resolver that remains answers this the other way** - `Core/Array.yana` writes the pairs as
   ordinary bodies marked `@inline(always)`, grafted at SSA conversion with no optimizer involved,
   so the cost argument above is met rather than dodged; `Core/Pointer.native.yana` has the third.

   **The attribute was not what stood between the two** - inventory D12, 2026-09-06. The old stage's
   graft took only a single expression with no branch, no call and no field read, and what it
   declined was admitted by `compiler/opt`'s inliner
   against its ceiling alone rather than reported as an error, so an `@inline(always)` owner-head
   `Index` measured identical or shorter at `-mode lower` than its emitters did.
   `Length(Array(a))` is written in `Core/Array.yana` and carries the attribute for that reason.

   What stood there instead was the **borrow checker**, and it is a fact about `getMut` rather than
   about cost: a written member hands back a borrow the signature roots in `self`, so a call there
   took an exclusive loan of the whole container for as long as the element lived, where the
   emitters produced a borrow of the *element's* address. `xs[k] = xs[k] + 1` and a subscript handed
   to a lens were then refused. `Core/Array.yana` carries the measurement and the three shapes; here
   an element borrow is a place, and J173 retired the emitters with the stage that needed them.

   One instance serves the owner and the window, which is not an omission: a window and the
   container it is over are one logical type here, so the instance over `Array(a)` is the window's
   too and nothing converts at the call - it serves `[a]`, `Array(a)` and every window taken of one.
   The class buys the *generic* case - `xs[i]` inside
   `fn (Index(c, k, v)) first(xs: c)` - and costs the concrete one nothing.

   The pointer instance is Implementation-Containers.md §17's "two declarations and one deletion":
   `heapFree[sizeClass]` was `store(heapFree + sizeClass, block)` because a subscript once rejected
   anything that was not an array, and under the class route it is an instance like any other with
   the unchecked-ness the pointer's rather than the subscript's.

   None of this is what a *user's* container gets, deliberately. An authored `instance Index(Deque(a),
   Int, a)` is an ordinary function dispatched to exactly as now, and what would make one of those
   free is `@inline(always)` - a guarantee written on the declaration, paid for where it is asked for.
   These six are the compiler's own types and are in every program, which is the whole of why they are
   answered here instead.
-}
